title: 'Token files are read once: a client outlives the rotation of the token it
  authenticates with'
updated: 2026-08-13T17:35:51Z
---

A bearer token that comes from a file -- a user tokenFile, or the service account token the in-cluster fallback reads from /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token -- is read once, while Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig->api builds the client, and copied into a Kubernetes::REST::AuthToken, whose token attribute is a plain immutable Str. The Kubernetes::REST instance then carries that one string for its whole life.

Kubernetes rotates both files. The kubelet rewrites a projected service account token well before it expires (the default is an hour, and it is rewritten at 80% of the lifetime), and client-go copes by re-reading: NewCachedFileTokenSource wraps the file in a cachingTokenSource with period one minute, and the comment there says outright that the period is chosen to match how Kubernetes refreshes projected tokens. bearerAuthRoundTripper asks that source on every request.

We do not, so a long-running process -- an operator, a watch loop, anything that builds one client at startup and keeps it -- goes on sending the token it read at startup and starts collecting 401s once it expires, while the file next to it holds a valid one. Nothing in the error says "your token is stale", and it only shows up after an hour of running, which is the worst shape a bug can have.

The natural place is the credentials side, not the kubeconfig side: Kubernetes::REST calls $self->credentials->token for every request (see prepare_request), and the credentials attribute already accepts any object with a token() method. So a Kubernetes::REST::AuthTokenFile -- file path, a cached token, a max age after which the next token() call re-reads, keeping the last good content if a read fails mid-rotation -- drops in without touching the pipeline. Kubeconfig::api would hand back one of those for a user with a tokenFile, and _in_cluster_api one for the service account token, instead of an AuthToken holding a string.

Two things to decide when picking this up: whether the re-read interval is fixed (client-go: one minute) or configurable, and whether a read failure after a successful first read is fatal or keeps the previous token -- client-go keeps the previous one, which is the right call during a rotation that briefly leaves the file half-written.

New public class, so: its own file with its own $VERSION and ABSTRACT, POD, a Changes bullet, and t/ coverage that fakes a rotation by rewriting the file between two requests. The limitation is documented in the meantime under TOKEN FILES AND ROTATION in Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig; that section shrinks to a pointer when this lands.
